\chapter{\texttt{Maybe} and \texttt{Result}: Monadic Error Handling}
\label{ch:maybe-and-result}

\section{Two Ways to Say "It Did Not Work"}

A DSL that parses, rewrites, or evaluates has to represent two kinds of
failure, and they are not the same kind. The first is the absence of
something: the input contained no identifier, the rule did not apply, the
optional field was not present. The second is the failure of something: the
token was unterminated, the rewrite diverged, the task exited with a
non-zero status. The first is usually not an error at all; it is a normal
outcome that the caller handles by supplying a default. The second is an
error that the caller must be told about, in enough detail to diagnose it
and to decide what to do.

\DSLtk{} provides one type for each. \code{dsl::Maybe<T>} is a container
that may or may not hold a value. \code{dsl::Result<T, E>} holds either a
value of type \code{T} or an error of type \code{E}. Both offer a small
algebra of transformations -- \code{map}, \code{flat_map}, \code{filter},
\code{and_then} -- that compose without the caller having to test for
absence or failure at every step.

The word \emph{monadic} in this chapter's title is not decoration. Both
types support the operations that make a monad a monad, and
Section~\ref{sec:ch20-laws} explains what those operations must satisfy
for the composition to be predictable. The implementations in the shipped
header are compact enough to read in a few minutes, and they depart from
the more familiar \code{std::optional} and \code{std::expected} in ways
that are worth knowing before they are used.

\section{A Worked Example}

Listing~\ref{lst:ch20-both} is a complete program that exercises both
types, including the failure paths.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={Both error-handling types in one program. \code{Maybe} models the absence of a value; \code{Result} models the failure of a step. The final two lines show that the free factories \code{Ok} and \code{Err} produce plain wrapper structs, not \code{Result} objects.},label={lst:ch20-both}]
#include "DSLtk.hpp"
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <variant>

struct ParseError
{
  std::string message;
};

int main ()
{
  std::cout << std::boolalpha;

  // ------------------------- Maybe -------------------------
  dsl::Maybe<int> some{ 42 };
  dsl::Maybe<int> none;

  std::cout << "some.has_value() = " << some.has_value () << "\n";
  std::cout << "none.has_value() = " << none.has_value () << "\n";
  std::cout << "some.as_optional().value_or(-1) = "
            << some.as_optional ().value_or (-1) << "\n";

  auto doubled = some.map ([] (int x) { return x * 2; });
  std::cout << "some.map(x->2x).or_else(-1)  = " << doubled.or_else (-1)
            << "\n";
  std::cout << "none.map(x->2x).or_else(-1)  = "
            << none.map ([] (int x) { return x * 2; }).or_else (-1) << "\n";

  auto positive = some.filter ([] (int x) { return x > 0; });
  std::cout << "some.filter(x>0).has_value() = " << positive.has_value ()
            << "\n";
  auto negative = some.filter ([] (int x) { return x < 0; });
  std::cout << "some.filter(x<0).has_value() = " << negative.has_value ()
            << "\n";

  auto nested = some.flat_map (
      [] (int x) -> dsl::Maybe<double> {
        return x > 0 ? dsl::Maybe<double>{ x * 1.5 } : dsl::Maybe<double>{};
      });
  std::cout << "some.flat_map(...)             = " << nested.or_else (0.0)
            << "\n";

  // ------------------------- Result -------------------------
  using R = dsl::Result<int, ParseError>;

  R ok = R::from_ok (7);
  R bad = R::from_err (ParseError{ "unterminated token" });

  std::cout << "ok.is_ok()   = " << ok.is_ok () << "\n";
  std::cout << "bad.is_err() = " << bad.is_err () << "\n";
  std::cout << "ok.unwrap()  = " << ok.unwrap () << "\n";
  std::cout << "bad.unwrap_or(-1) = " << bad.unwrap_or (-1) << "\n";
  std::cout << "ok.map(x->x+1).unwrap_or(-1)   = "
            << ok.map ([] (int x) { return x + 1; }).unwrap_or (-1) << "\n";
  std::cout << "bad.map(x->x+1).unwrap_or(-1)  = "
            << bad.map ([] (int x) { return x + 1; }).unwrap_or (-1) << "\n";

  auto renamed = bad.map_err (
      [] (const ParseError &e) { return e.message.size (); });
  std::cout << "bad.map_err(...).is_err()      = " << renamed.is_err ()
            << "\n";

  R chained = ok.and_then (
      [] (int x) -> R { return R::from_ok (x * 10); });
  std::cout << "ok.and_then(x->10x).unwrap()   = " << chained.unwrap ()
            << "\n";

  R chained2 = bad.and_then (
      [] (int x) -> R { return R::from_ok (x * 10); });
  std::cout << "bad.and_then(x->10x).is_err()  = " << chained2.is_err ()
            << "\n";

  try
    {
      (void) bad.unwrap ();
    }
  catch (const std::runtime_error &e)
    {
      std::cout << "runtime_error: " << e.what () << "\n";
    }

  // The free factories produce plain wrappers, not Results.
  auto okw = dsl::Ok (5);
  auto errw = dsl::Err (ParseError{ "boom" });
  std::cout << "dsl::Ok(5).value   = " << okw.value << "\n";
  std::cout << "dsl::Err(...).error= " << errw.error.message << "\n";
}
\end{lstlisting}

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcmd,caption={Output of Listing~\ref{lst:ch20-both}. Notice that \code{map} on an absent \code{Maybe} produces an absent result rather than a default, and that \code{and_then} on an \code{Err} leaves the error in place without ever calling the supplied function.},label={lst:ch20-both-out}]
some.has_value() = true
none.has_value() = false
some.as_optional().value_or(-1) = 42
some.map(x->2x).or_else(-1)  = 84
none.map(x->2x).or_else(-1)  = -1
some.filter(x>0).has_value() = true
some.filter(x<0).has_value() = false
some.flat_map(...)             = 63
ok.is_ok()   = true
bad.is_err() = true
ok.unwrap()  = 7
bad.unwrap_or(-1) = -1
ok.map(x->x+1).unwrap_or(-1)   = 8
bad.map(x->x+1).unwrap_or(-1)  = -1
bad.map_err(...).is_err()      = true
ok.and_then(x->10x).unwrap()   = 70
bad.and_then(x->10x).is_err()  = true
runtime_error: dsl::Result::unwrap called on Err
dsl::Ok(5).value   = 5
dsl::Err(...).error= boom
\end{lstlisting}

\section{\code{Maybe}: The Shape of the Type}

\code{dsl::Maybe<T>} (lines 7451--7510) is a thin wrapper around a
\code{std::optional<T>}. Its private member is exactly that: a
\code{std::optional<T> value_}. Everything else is a method.

The constructors are a default constructor, one taking a \code{T}, and one
taking a \code{std::optional<T>}. All three are implicit, so
\code{dsl::Maybe<int> m = 5;} compiles, as does
\code{dsl::Maybe<int> m = std::optional<int>{5};}.

The members are \code{has_value()}, which is \code{const noexcept} and
reports presence; \code{as_optional()}, which returns a
\code{const std::optional<T> &}; and the four transformations \code{map},
\code{flat_map}, \code{filter} and \code{or_else}.

\subsection{What Is Missing, and Why It Matters}

The following are \emph{absent} from \code{dsl::Maybe<T>}:
\code{some}, \code{none}, \code{map_or}, \code{and_then},
\code{value_or}, \code{operator bool}, \code{operator*} and
\code{operator->}.

Two of these absences have consequences that run through the whole type.

Because there is no \code{operator bool}, a \code{Maybe} cannot be used
directly in an \code{if}. The compiler's diagnostic is
\code{"could not convert 'm' from 'dsl::Maybe<int>' to 'bool'"}. The
programmer must write \code{if (m.has_value())}. This is a deliberate
choice in most designs, and it is the right one for a type whose whole
purpose is to make the empty case explicit, but it does mean that a
\code{Maybe} cannot be dropped into a context that expects a truth value.

Because there is no \code{operator*} and no \code{operator->}, a
\code{Maybe} cannot be dereferenced. The value must be reached through
\code{or_else} (which copies it out) or through \code{as_optional()}
(which returns the underlying \code{optional}, on which \code{operator*}
does exist). The second route is the only one that avoids a copy, and it is
also the only one that gives access to the value without providing a
fallback.

The absence of \code{value_or} and \code{map_or} is a matter of naming:
\code{or_else} plays the role of \code{value_or}, and there is no
counterpart to \code{map_or} because \code{map} followed by
\code{or_else} expresses the same thing.

\subsection{The \code{map} Subtlety}

\code{Maybe::map} (lines 7474--7482) has a declaration and a body that
disagree slightly about value category, and the disagreement is worth
understanding because it constrains what the callable may look like.

The declared return type is shown in Listing~\ref{lst:ch20-decls}. The
argument to \code{std::declval<T>()} is an \emph{rvalue} of type
\code{T}, so the declared type is computed from calling \code{fn} on an
rvalue.

The body, however, computes the type of \code{fn(*value_)}. The operand of
\code{*value_} is a \code{const T &}, so that is a call on a \emph{const
lvalue}, and the value is in fact passed to the function as a const lvalue.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={The three declared return types that share the same value-category subtlety: \code{Maybe::map}, \code{Maybe::flat_map} and \code{Result::and_then}, transcribed from \code{DSLtk.hpp}. Each is computed from a call of the supplied function on an \emph{rvalue} of the value type, while the bodies call the function on a \emph{const lvalue}.},label={lst:ch20-decls}]
  // dsl::Maybe<T>::map, line 7476
  auto map (Fn &&fn) const -> Maybe<decltype (fn (std::declval<T> ()))>

  // dsl::Maybe<T>::flat_map, line 7485
  auto flat_map (Fn &&fn) const -> decltype (fn (std::declval<T> ()))

  // dsl::Result<T, E>::and_then, line 7593
  auto and_then (Fn &&fn) const -> decltype (fn (std::declval<T> ()))
\end{lstlisting}

The two agree for the overwhelming majority of callables: anything taking
\code{T} by value or \code{const T &} yields the same type either way.
They disagree for a callable that takes a non-const \code{T &}. In that
case the \emph{declaration} is what fails, because a non-const lvalue
reference cannot bind to the rvalue that \code{std::declval<T>()}
produces. The compiler's diagnostic is explicit:
\code{"cannot bind non-const lvalue reference of type 'int&' to an rvalue
of type 'int'"}. So \code{map} cannot be used with a lambda that mutates
its argument in place; such a lambda must take its argument by value.

\code{flat_map} has the same shape, with one addition: its body names the
chained type \code{R} and returns \code{R{}} when the cell is empty. The
empty case therefore requires that \code{R} be default-constructible, which
is true of \code{Maybe<U>} but not of every type a caller might want to
chain into.

\subsection{The Free Helpers Are Not for \code{Maybe}}

The namespace also contains four free functions, \code{map},
\code{flat_map}, \code{filter} and \code{or_else} (lines 7511--7541).
They are generic over a parameter named \code{Opt}, and they look as though
they should work with \code{Maybe}. They do not.

\code{dsl::map} is declared over a parameter named \code{Opt}, and it
dereferences its argument with \code{*opt} while testing it for truth with
\code{!opt}. A \code{Maybe} has neither \code{operator*} nor
\code{operator bool}, so the call does not compile; the diagnostic names
\code{operator*} on \code{dsl::Maybe<int>} and says there is no match. The
return type is \code{std::optional<...>} regardless of \code{Opt}, so even
if the body compiled the result would not be a \code{Maybe}.

These helpers are for \code{std::optional}, and they exist because
\code{std::optional} has no member \code{map} of its own before \cpp{23}.
The D port has the same four functions, with the same restriction: they are
declared over \code{Nullable!T}. In the \cpp{} port they are used by the
shipped example \code{20-monadic.cpp}, which applies them to a
\code{std::optional<int>} and not to a \code{Maybe}.

\section{\code{Result}: Success or Failure}

\code{dsl::Result<T, E>} (lines 7543--7636) stores its payload in a
\code{std::variant<T, E>}. The variant is held in a private member
\code{data_}, and the alternative in use determines everything else.

The two constructors are both implicit. The first constructs the variant
with the success alternative in place; the second constructs it with the
error alternative in place. The second is constrained by
\code{requires (!std::same_as<T, E>)}, which is the source of the type's
most significant limitation, discussed below.

The present API is \code{from_ok} and \code{from_err}, both static
factories; \code{is_ok()} and \code{is_err()}; \code{unwrap()} and
\code{unwrap_or()}; and the three transformations \code{map},
\code{map_err} and \code{and_then}.

The following are \emph{absent}: \code{ok()}, \code{err()}, \code{value()},
\code{error()}, \code{expect()} and \code{value_or()}. The absence of
\code{ok()} and \code{err()} means that the payload of a \code{Result}
cannot be extracted without either unwrapping or transforming it; there is
no way to ask "give me the error if there is one, and give me nothing
otherwise". The absence of \code{expect()} means there is no way to attach
a diagnostic message to a failure, which is a real limitation for a parser,
where the useful information is usually a position and a set of expected
tokens.

\code{unwrap()} is the escape hatch. It returns the value, or throws
\code{std::runtime_error} with the fixed message
\code{"dsl::Result::unwrap called on Err"}. The message names the type and
the operation and nothing else: it does not include the error payload, so a
caller that catches it learns that a failure occurred but not what it was.

\code{and_then} (lines 7591--7599) is the interesting one; its declared
return type appears in Listing~\ref{lst:ch20-decls}. The callable must
itself return something \code{Result}-shaped. The body then needs to
construct an error of that type, and it does so by calling
\code{R::from_err}. The requirement that falls out of this is that the
callable's return type \code{R} must have a static \code{from_err} that
accepts an \code{E}. For the natural case, where \code{R} is
\code{Result<U, E>} with the same error type, this is satisfied
automatically. For a chain that changes the error type, it is not, and the
call does not compile unless the new error type is implicitly constructible
from the old one.

\code{map_err} (lines 7608--7616) is the opposite transformation: it
leaves a success value alone and applies the function to an error,
producing a \code{Result<T, E2>} with a different error type. It is the
mechanism by which a low-level error type is refined into a richer one as
the failure propagates outwards.

\subsection{The \code{T == E} Restriction}

The constraint \code{requires (!std::same_as<T, E>)} removes the ambiguous
error constructor when payload types coincide. Indexed factories can still
construct either alternative, and \code{std::variant<T, E>} permits repeated
types. Normal result inspection fails because \code{std::get<T>} and
\code{std::holds_alternative<T>} both require the alternative to occur
exactly once.

This is not a theoretical restriction. The \code{dsl::Task} type at line
8002 and the \code{dsl::TaskState} type at line 7993 both use
\code{Result<std::string, std::string>}, and the task pipeline therefore
does not compile. The failure surfaces at \code{dsl::run}, at line 8040,
which calls \code{result.is_err()}; that in turn calls
\code{std::holds_alternative<E>} on a
\code{std::variant<std::string, std::string>}, and the standard library
rejects it with the static assertion "T must occur exactly once in
alternatives". Chapter~\ref{ch:task-pipeline} takes this apart in detail.

The D port does not have the restriction. Its \code{Result} stores a
boolean flag alongside the two payloads rather than a variant, and its
comment says so: "Explicitly tagged storage also supports identical
success/error types."

\subsection{The \code{Ok} and \code{Err} Markers}

The header also declares two aggregate structs and two free factories
(lines 7637--7656):

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={The \code{OkWrap} and \code{ErrWrap} markers and their factories, transcribed from \code{DSLtk.hpp} lines 7637--7656. In the \cpp{} port they are plain aggregates with no relationship to \code{Result}; in the D port they are the constructors of \code{Result}.},label={lst:ch20-wraps}]
template <typename T> struct OkWrap
{
  T value;
};
template <typename E> struct ErrWrap
{
  E error;
};
template <typename T>
OkWrap<T>
Ok (T value)
{
  return { std::move (value) };
}
template <typename E>
ErrWrap<E>
Err (E error)
{
  return { std::move (error) };
}
\end{lstlisting}

In the \cpp{} port these are \emph{unambiguous markers with no conversion
to \code{Result}}. There is no constructor from \code{OkWrap<T>} to
\code{Result<T, E>}, no assignment operator, and no implicit conversion of
any kind. Writing \code{dsl::Result<int, ParseError> r = dsl::Ok (5);} is
a compile error. The markers can only be used as markers: as a way of
naming the two cases in an interface of one's own, or as a temporary
container from which the payload is extracted by hand.

The D port uses them for something more substantial. There,
\code{Result} has constructors from \code{OkWrap!T} and \code{ErrWrap!E},
and its \code{from_ok} and \code{from_err} are implemented in terms of
them (\code{DSLtk.d}, lines 576--577 and 606--607). The tags there solve
the same ambiguity problem that the \cpp{} port solves with the constrained
constructor, and they solve it in a way that also permits
\code{T == E}.

\subsection{Chaining Results}

Figure~\ref{fig:ch20-andthen} draws the shape of a chain. Along the upper
lane, each step receives the previous step's value and produces a new
\code{Result}. Along the lower lane, an error skips every step untouched.

