\label{fig:ch20-andthen}
\end{figure}

Algorithm~\ref{alg:ch20-propagate} states the rule that the implementation
follows, and it is the rule that makes \code{and_then} different from
\code{map}.

\begin{pseudocode}[Propagating an error through a chain]
\textbf{function} and\_then (result, step)\\
\quad \textbf{if} result carries an error then\\
\qquad \textbf{return} an error carrying the same payload\\
\quad \textbf{return} step (the value carried by result)
\label{alg:ch20-propagate}
\end{pseudocode}

The distinction between \code{map} and \code{and_then} is the distinction
between transforming a value and running a fallible step. \code{map}
applies its function to the value and wraps the outcome back into the same
shape; the function cannot fail. \code{and_then} applies its function to
the value and takes the outcome as it is, which means the function can
itself fail, and the failure is propagated rather than flattened. In
functional terms, \code{map} is the functorial operation and
\code{and_then} is the monadic one.

\section{The Monadic Laws, Plainly}
\label{sec:ch20-laws}

The header's own comment on \code{Maybe} says that "Monadic laws
(left identity/right identity/associativity) apply when map/flat\_map
functions are pure and side-effect free." That is the right caveat: the
laws are properties of the composition, not of the container, and they hold
only when the functions involved are deterministic.

The three laws, in plain terms, are these. \emph{Left identity}: wrapping a
value and then unwrapping it changes nothing.
\code{map(f, Just(x))} must equal \code{Just(f(x))}, and
\code{and_then(f, Just(x))} must equal \code{f(x)}. \emph{Right identity}:
handing a function that does nothing but return its input must not change
the value, so \code{map(f, Just(x))} must equal \code{Just(x)} when
\code{f} is the identity, and \code{and_then(id, r)} must equal \code{r}.
\emph{Associativity}: two ways of chaining three steps must agree.
\code{and_then(g, and_then(f, r))} must equal
\code{and_then(compose(g, f), r)}.

For \code{Maybe}, all three hold by inspection of the implementation, with
one caveat that the laws do not mention: the empty case propagates
through \code{map} and \code{and_then} alike, and \code{filter} adds a
fourth property, idempotence, which says that filtering twice is the same
as filtering once. \code{filter} is not part of the monad; it is a
convenience that happens to be lawful.

For \code{Result}, the same three laws hold with the error payload playing
the role of the empty case, and they hold for \code{map} and
\code{and_then} together, since \code{and_then} is the one that
propagates errors unchanged. The one law that is \emph{not} automatic is
the error side of left identity: \code{map(f, Err(e))} must produce an
error carrying the same \code{e}. It does, because the body of \code{map}
constructs the error branch with \code{Result<U, E>::from_err(std::get<E>
(data_))}, which copies the payload across unchanged.

The practical value of the laws is compositional: if each step of a chain
obeys them, then the chain as a whole is predictable, and a programmer can
refactor a chain without changing its meaning. The practical cost is that
the laws constrain the functions used with \code{map} and
\code{and_then} to be pure; a function with a side effect that is applied
inside a chain will not be applied at all when the chain short-circuits, and
the laws do not describe that behaviour.

