The comparison shows what \DSLtk{} gained and what it left out. Compared
with \code{std::optional}, \code{Maybe} adds a fluent transformation
vocabulary and nothing else: it has no truth test, no dereference, and no
way to read the value without either a fallback or a copy. Compared with
\code{std::expected}, \code{Result} adds nothing at all in terms of
surface area, and it lacks the two features that matter most in practice,
namely \code{error()} and \code{expect()}. Its contribution is that it
exists in \cpp{20} and that it composes with the rest of the toolkit.

\section{Edge Cases and Limits}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{\code{Maybe} cannot be tested or dereferenced.} There is no
\code{operator bool} and no \code{operator*}, so a \code{Maybe} cannot
appear in an \code{if} condition and cannot be starred. The only ways to
reach the value are \code{or_else}, which copies it out, and
\code{as_optional()}, which exposes the underlying \code{optional}.

\item \textbf{The free helpers do not accept \code{Maybe}.} They require
\code{*opt} and truthiness, which \code{Maybe} does not have, and their
return types are \code{std::optional} rather than \code{Maybe}.

\item \textbf{\code{Maybe::map} cannot take a mutable lvalue reference.}
The declared return type is computed from a call on an rvalue, so a
callable taking \code{T &} fails to compile even though the body would
have been fine.

\item \code{flat_map} requires the chained type to be
default-constructible, because the empty branch returns \code{R{}}.

\item \textbf{Identical success and error types cannot be queried.}
Construction is possible, but type-based variant access requires a unique
alternative. The shipped task types use this specialization, preventing
normal driver instantiation.

\item \textbf{\code{unwrap()} throws a message that omits the error.} The
message is fixed by the header, and it names the type and the operation but
not the payload. There is no \code{expect()} with which to attach a
diagnostic of one's own.

\item \textbf{\code{and_then} constrains the error type of the chain.}
The callable's return type must have a static \code{from_err} accepting
the current error type. Chaining to a \code{Result} with a different error
type does not compile unless the new type is implicitly constructible from
the old one.

\item \textbf{\code{Ok} and \code{Err} do not build a \code{Result}.}
They are plain aggregates in the \cpp{} port. A \code{Result} must be built
with \code{from_ok} or \code{from_err}, or with the implicit
constructors.

\item \textbf{Neither type is thread-safe, and neither is copy-cheap.}
\code{Maybe} copies its \code{optional} on every copy; \code{Result} copies
a \code{variant}. A \code{Result} carrying a large payload is copied on
every \code{map}, because \code{map} constructs a new \code{Result} with
the transformed value.

\item \textbf{The parser combinators do not use \code{Result}.}
\code{dsl::ExpectedResult<T>} (line 7717) is a separate type with its own
error structure: a \code{std::optional<T>} value, a \code{dsl::ParseError}
carrying a position, a failure kind and a list of expected tokens, and an
\code{explicit operator bool}. It is a richer type than \code{Result} for
the parsing case, and it is not built on \code{Result}.
\end{itemize}

\section{Closing}

\code{Maybe} and \code{Result} are small types with large ambitions. The
implementations fit in a couple of hundred lines, and the transformations
they support -- \code{map}, \code{flat_map}, \code{filter},
\code{and_then}, \code{map_err} -- are the ones that make a chain of
fallible steps expressible without a test at every step. What they do not
offer is the ergonomics of the standard library: no truth test, no
dereference, no error accessor, no custom failure message. A programmer
coming from Rust's \code{Option} and \code{Result}, or from \cpp{23}'s
\code{std::expected}, will find the shapes familiar and the conveniences
missing.

The next chapter begins the parsing half of the toolkit, with the input
stream, the error type, and the parser combinator that ties them together.
